{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-58819","model_call":42,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"school tracking harms millions\" dornbusch)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"school tracking harms millions\" dornbusch)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#5_1018227938","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1539","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#5_1018227938\" , \"end_char\": 6346.000000 , \"headings\": \"<cover>SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS\" , \"segment\": \"\\\"This finding upsets me,\\\" Dornbusch </cover>said. \" This set of data points to a  systemic pattern of ignorance, and African Americans and Hispanics are even  less aware of the extent to which the tracking system is short-changing them. These results help us to understand why so many talented and hard-working  minority students are ineligible for four-year colleges and universities. \"And of course, the paucity of these minorities among scientists and  engineers is a necessary consequence.\" But, he added, \"if one fifth of non-Hispanic whites and Asians are also  misinformed, we are discussing a major national problem that does not just  affect disadvantaged minorities.\" Some of the findings were foreshadowed in an earlier study by Dornbusch. In four Northern California high schools, he found that 55 percent of the  students could not correctly state even one admission requirement for the  University of California, and 76 percent could not identify a single  California State University ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#5_1018227938","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":29},"model_calls":42,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
