{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-58819","model_call":46,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":17285,"output_tokens":422,"total_tokens":17707,"cached_tokens":16128,"reasoning_tokens":212},"output_characters":923,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","relevance":2,"reason":"The full Stanford segment is directly related to educational misinformation because it says college-aspiring students and parents often do not know students have been tracked out of college-preparatory math/science, and it introduces the 1,200-student Stanford tracking study, but this segment does not give the Latino-specific 'how many' answer.","learned":"The Stanford study analyzed records for about 1,200 students in six diverse San Francisco Bay Area high schools, sampled equally by gender-ethnicity and grades, and found tracking can be hidden from students and parents."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539","reason":"The title-and-researcher query is yielding consecutive Stanford article segments; continuing may reveal any remaining unjudged segments with direct Latino/Hispanic figures or supporting evidence."}}}
