{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-58819","model_call":89,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1576"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1576"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_1473773477#4_1668486622","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1576","rank":7,"snippet":"championed equal educational opportunity as a Lincoln High faculty member, students at Lincoln organized a mass walk-out in protest of sub-standard facilities, vocational program <cover>tracking for Chicano youth and discriminatory practices which excluded them from advanced college prep courses. In March 1968, Lincoln High students led the first wave of what became the largest student strike in the history of public education in US. The \"Blow-out\" was joined by students from at least three other area high schools, among them Garfield, Roosevelt, Lincoln, Belmont and Wilson. In 2009 the opening of the Felicitas and Gonzalo Mendez Learning Centers relieved Lincoln. In 2015, Lincoln High School's baseball team won the CIF Los Angeles City Section Division II baseball championship, defeating Cesar Chavez High School of San Fernando by a score of 3-0 in a game played at Dodger Stadium. It was the school's second baseball championship, and the first one since 1935. Demographics Demographics Demographics of student body Ethnic Breakdown 2020 2019 Native Americans 0.3% 0.4% Hispanic </cover>and Latino American 75% 73% Black 2% 1% Asian American 21% 23% Native Hawaiian or other Pacific Islander 0% 0% White 1% 1% Multiracial Americans 1%","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#3_1018223792","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#8_1018233542","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#4_1018225918","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#2_1018221721","rank":5,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_